  "answer_text": "Making eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\" (colloquially used to describe **strabismus**, where the eyes are misaligned and one may point in a different direction) can sometimes feel socially uncertain. You want to show respect and attention without making the other person feel self-conscious. \n\nThe most effective, polite, and natural ways to handle eye contact in this situation include:\n\n### 1. Focus on the eye that is looking at you\nWhen speaking with someone with strabismus, one eye is usually the \"dominant\" or \"fixing\" eye that is actively focusing on you, while the other eye drifts. **Look directly at the eye that is making contact with yours.** This is the eye they are using to see you, and focusing on it will feel like completely normal, respectful eye contact for both of you.\n\n### 2. Look at the bridge of their nose\nIf you find yourself getting distracted, unsure of which eye to focus on, or feeling like your own gaze is wavering, focus on **the bridge of their nose** (right between their eyes). \n* To the other person, looking at the bridge of the nose is virtually indistinguishable from direct eye contact. \n* This technique relieves any pressure on you to \"choose\" an eye, ensuring you aren't staring at their wandering eye.\n\n### 3. Avoid \"shifty\" eyes (darting back and forth)\nWhat makes many people with strabismus uncomfortable is when they see their conversational partner's eyes rapidly darting back and forth between their left and right eyes. They can easily tell when you are trying to \"figure out\" where to look, which can make them feel highly self-conscious. Pick one spot\u2014either the aligned eye or the bridge of the nose\u2014and stick with it.\n\n### 4. Keep eye contact natural (don't overcompensate)\nYou do not need to maintain intense, unbroken eye contact. In normal human interaction, we rarely stare continuously. \n* Use the **50/70 rule**: Make eye contact about 50% of the time while you are speaking, and 70% of the time while you are listening.\n* Break eye contact naturally by looking slightly away when you are thinking, nodding, or gesturing. This keeps the interaction feeling relaxed and unforced.\n\n### 5. Focus on the conversation, not the eyes\nUltimately, the best approach is to treat the conversation normally. People with strabismus are highly aware of their condition and are used to navigating social interactions. If you focus entirely on *what* they are saying rather than *where* they are looking, your body language will naturally relax, and the interaction will be comfortable for both of you.",
